{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2183 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "814c959af8960479344b2a8c465bc32e3460a4eb40ac2d33187db2a350afc76f" :edb/db-before-t 2182 :edb/db-after-t 2183 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535495 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:13:35.253Z" 13194139535495 true] [17592186151834 1100 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n\n- $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n\n- $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" 13194139535495 true] [17592186151834 1100 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n1. $y'' = x\\cos(x), \\quad y(2)=7$\n2. $y'' + 3y = x^2+1, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y(5)=0$\n3. $2y'' - y' = e^{2x}, \\quad y(-1)=1, \\quad y(1)=3$" 13194139535495 false] [17592186151834 1145 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y$, then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." 13194139535495 false] [17592186151834 1145 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." 13194139535495 true] [17592186151835 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0,  \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/c1e4a45a8051dccc92a71ca2cf0b3851cfb5c6c5c22e2fc416ebcc8aeba96740.png).\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/077041b590cf1030028330dae6748828deef81c3d8d6a1c70994a90407ac05a2.png) boundary conditions are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/50f935ce3a16bbb94697f74737b4bba66dc9ce01d6629e007ff64f46347c1ccb.png)\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066273/assets/339c256afc594fe366f0fdcb008556ef43b33be529fed25c65e7faa077166be2.png)." 13194139535495 true] [17592186151835 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0, \\qquad 1\\leq x \\leq 3$\n\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\quad$ The governing differential equation is $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ For this BVP, the $AAAAA$ boundary conditions are $AAAAA$\n\n$\\quad$ The boundary value problem is $AAAAA$." 13194139535495 false] [17592186151835 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)\n$$\n\nOtherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n$y''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0$.\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0$, the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}$.\n- The boundary conditions are\n$y(1) = 0, \\qquad y(3) = -5$.\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$.\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}$." 13194139535495 false] [17592186151835 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., $\\gamma_1 = \\gamma_2 = 0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f \\equiv 0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\ny''(x) + e^x y'(x) - y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x) \\equiv 0,$ the equation is $\\textrm{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(1) = 0, \\qquad\ny(3) = -5.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\textrm{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\textrm{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139535495 true] [17592186151836 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny''(x) + 2y(x) = x,  \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n- Robin\n\n- Dirichlet\n\n- Neumann\n\n- Periodic" 13194139535495 true] [17592186151836 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y''(x) + 2y(x) = x, \\qquad y(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1, \\qquad 0\\leq x \\leq 2$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?\n\n1. Robin\n2. Dirichlet\n3. Neumann\n4. Periodic" 13194139535495 false] [17592186151836 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem,\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b]$, we have the following boundary condition classifications.\n\n- Dirichlet:\n$y(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Neumann:\n$y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Robin (mixed):\n$\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2$\n- Periodic:\n$y(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a) = y'(b)$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i$, and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2$.\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." 13194139535495 false] [17592186151836 1145 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y'(x) + Q(x)y(x) = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\ny'(a)  = \\gamma_1, \\qquad y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a) = y(b), \\qquad y'(a)  = y'(b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma_i, \\alpha_i,$ and $\\beta_i$ are real numbers with $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x = a = 0$ and $x = b = 2.$\n\n$$\ny(0) - y'(0) = 4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." 13194139535495 true] [17592186152206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351986 13194139535495 true] [17592186152206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351993 13194139535495 true] [17592186152206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352000 13194139535495 true] [17592186152206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352007 13194139535495 true] [17592186152206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352119 13194139535495 true] [17592186152207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352014 13194139535495 true] [17592186152207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352021 13194139535495 true] [17592186152207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352028 13194139535495 true] [17592186152207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352035 13194139535495 true] [17592186152207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352042 13194139535495 true] [17592186152208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352049 13194139535495 true] [17592186152208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352063 13194139535495 true] [17592186152208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352077 13194139535495 true] [17592186152208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352091 13194139535495 true] [17592186152208 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352105 13194139535495 true] [17592186351986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351987 13194139535495 true] [17592186351986 1089 "q-130997" 13194139535495 true] [17592186351986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535495 true] [17592186351986 1100 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+y=x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=xy,\\,y(0)=3,\\,y(1)=4$\n- $2{y}^{″}+{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" 13194139535495 true] [17592186351986 1113 #uuid "41bb21e9-bc52-542d-b300-e51317f5152f" 13194139535495 true] [17592186351986 1145 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$)." 131941395